# The export destination is the user's to choose, and the export goes there.
#
# "Export all" used to write to ~/hanabi/threads and only there. The row now
# names the chosen folder and offers "Choose…", which opens the native folder
# picker (native_pick_directory / NSOpenPanel).
#
# WHAT THIS PROVES, AND WHAT IT CANNOT. A modal NSOpenPanel is not in the
# widget tree: a script that clicked "Choose…" for real would stop against a
# panel nothing can dismiss. So HANABI_PICK_DIR_TEST answers the ask with a
# path, and everything the answer touches is exercised for real — the button,
# the persistence, the row that reads it back, and the destination the export
# actually writes to. The panel itself is verified by hand (see the theme
# report); this is the same split the drop test makes.
#
# Opened through the state knob because the harness cannot press Cmd+,
# (afterhours_gaps.md #49).
#
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":[],"active_tab":"","theme":"dark"}
# env: HANABI_TEST_OVERLAY=settings HANABI_PICK_DIR_TEST=/tmp/hanabi-export-choice

# The default destination, before anyone chooses anything.
expect_text "Export threads to ~/hanabi/threads"

click_ui settings_export_choose
wait_frames 6

# The row reads the chosen folder back — so the click reached Settings, and
# Settings is what the row asks.
expect_text "Export threads to /tmp/hanabi-export-choice"

# And the export goes to the chosen folder, not the default: the count is
# reported against the destination the row just named.
click_ui settings_export_btn
wait_frames 6
expect_text "threads to /tmp/hanabi-export-choice"
